<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cumans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polovtsians, Polovtsy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cumans were a Turkic nomadic people of the Eurasian steppe, closely linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> and called Polovtsians in Russian sources. From the mid-11th century they dominated the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, raiding and allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. After the Mongol invasion many fled to Hungary, where they settled as a privileged group, while others were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cumans.jpg" alt="Cumans" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cumanian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cumania_(1200)_eng.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cumans, or Kumans, formed the western branch of the Cuman–Kipchak confederation and spoke the Cuman language. They are called Polovtsy in the chronicles of the Rus', Cumans in Western sources and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> in Eastern ones. Their original homeland is unknown. The writer al-Marwazi, about 1120, says that a Turkic people called the Qun came from the northern borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and drove out the Śari people, and that they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christians</a>. Related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, the Cumans held a shifting area north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> known as Cumania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Cumans first met the Rus' in 1055. In 1061, under the chieftain Sokal, they devastated the Pereyaslavl principality, which began a war that lasted 175 years. At the Battle of the Alta River in 1068 they defeated the armies of the three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, and afterwards they repeatedly invaded the Rus' lands, taking captives who became slaves or were sold at markets in the south. Some Cumans were also allies: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV of Georgia</a> married the daughter of the Cuman king Atrak and invited her relatives to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> defeated the Cumans in 1220. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-kievan-rus">Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'</a> in 1237 many Cumans sought asylum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, where others had already settled. In 1239 and 1240 large groups crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and some were settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Those who stayed on the steppe joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and later adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Horses were central to Cuman life, and the main activity was animal husbandry. Robert de Clari described them as nomadic warriors who raised horses, sheep, goats, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, moving north with their herds in summer and south in winter. They fought as light cavalry, later developing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bows</a>, javelins, sabres and maces. Their religion was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism">Tengrism</a>, and the dead of high rank were buried under a mound with a horse and sometimes a servant or slave. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-cumanicus">Codex Cumanicus</a>, written between 1294 and 1356, was a manual to help Catholic missionaries communicate with them. Cuman names survive in places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumanovo">Kumanovo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cumans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1061 the <b>Cumans</b>, or Polovtsy, replaced them as the main steppe threat, routing the princes on the Alta River in 1068, until Vladimir Monomakh led joint campaigns deep into the steppe in 1103 and 1111.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pechenegs, Székelys and later <b>Cumans</b> served as frontier warriors, Muslim and Jewish merchants handled trade and sometimes royal finances, and from the twelfth century Germans, Walloons and Italians were invited as ‘guests’ (hospites) with their own liberties; Géza II settled the Saxons of Transylvania, whose rights Andrew II…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Late medieval Hungary was a large, thinly populated kingdom of probably around three million people by 1500, including Magyars, Slovaks, Croats, Romanians, Germans, Ruthenians and Serbs, as well as the Saxons of Transylvania and the Szeklers and <b>Cumans</b>, who held distinct collective rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The general Alexios Strategopoulos, sent with 800 soldiers, most of them <b>Cumans</b>, learned at Selymbria that the Latin garrison and the Venetian fleet were away raiding Daphnousia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslim writers drawing on 9th-century sources describe the Pecheneg territory between the Ural and Volga as a thirty-day walk across, bordered by the <b>Cumans</b>, Khazars, Oghuz Turks and Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongol invasion of 1241 ended the <b>Cumans</b>' domination without putting direct Mongol rule in its place, and the weakening of Hungarian authority during the Mongol attacks left room for new polities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
